# URL of the standalone y-websocket sync server (see ../server).
# Points at localhost for local dev; swap for the deployed Railway/Render URL in production.
NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL=ws://localhost:8080

# Clerk (accounts). Both come from the API Keys page of a Clerk application at
# dashboard.clerk.com. Signing in is optional for *users* — the guest flow needs
# no account — but set these to deploy, or nobody can sign in.
#
# `next build` still succeeds without them (proxy.ts doesn't run at build time),
# and `next dev` still boots because @clerk/nextjs falls back to keyless mode and
# provisions a throwaway instance under .clerk/ (gitignored). Measured on
# @clerk/nextjs 7.6.2: a production start with both keys removed AND .clerk/
# deleted still serves pages normally, so missing keys degrade auth rather than
# taking the site down. Don't reach for this as the cause of a 5xx.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Postgres (Neon). These are TWO connection strings to the SAME database, and
# swapping them produces confusing failures rather than an obvious error.
#
# DATABASE_URL is the *pooled* endpoint — its host contains `-pooler`. The app
# uses it at runtime (web/src/lib/data/db.ts) because Vercel runs many short-lived
# instances that would each open their own pool and exhaust the project's
# connection ceiling within a few requests.
#
# DIRECT_URL is the *unpooled* endpoint. Only `prisma migrate` uses it
# (prisma.config.ts). Neon's pooler runs pgbouncer in transaction mode, which
# cannot hold the session-level advisory lock a migration takes — point
# migrations at the pooled URL and they hang or fail mid-run.
#
# Both need TLS; Neon refuses plaintext. Prefer `?sslmode=verify-full` over the
# `?sslmode=require` Neon hands you: node-postgres currently treats `require` as
# an alias for `verify-full`, but warns that pg v9 will switch it to libpq
# semantics, which do NOT verify the certificate. Writing verify-full pins the
# strong behaviour so a routine dependency bump cannot quietly downgrade TLS.
#
# Nothing breaks in the live editor without these: sync, presence, Run and Save
# are all unchanged from v1 and never touch Postgres. Only the dead-room
# snapshot and /profile do.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxxx-pooler.region.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=verify-full
DIRECT_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxxx.region.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=verify-full
